# Facet — Controls

This file defines how a player operates the game: the pointer selects and swaps
gems on the board, and the keyboard drives a cursor over the same board and the
menus. The cell centers the pointer is measured against are in `specs/board.md`,
and the rules that decide whether a requested swap is accepted are in
`specs/rules.md`. Every figure below carries the name this specification gives
it.

## The pointer

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
This game stands on no engine, so the pointer belongs to the runtime layer you
write. What the game reads from that layer is a pointer position in the game's
logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`) stage, a press edge, and
a release edge. How the layer produces those three is yours to decide.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/` directory
at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a pointer
position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`)
stage, a press edge, and a release edge.
{{else}}
The pointer belongs to the engine, which documents it in the `engine/` directory
at the root of this repository. What the game reads from it is a pointer
position in the game's logical units on the `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`)
stage, a press edge, and a release edge, delivered as the ordered samples the
player controller reads from the engine each frame, so a drag that crossed
several cells between two frames arrives as every position it visited rather
than as the last one alone.
{{/if}}

The game acts on those three alone, and this file states what it does with them.
Each pointer position the game reads is resolved on its own, in the order the
positions arrive. The pointer drives the board on the `playing` screen, and menu
navigation runs through the actions listed at the end of this file.

## Selecting and swapping

A press targets the cell whose center is nearest the pointer position, when that
center lies within `GEM_HIT_R` (`36`) of it. `GEM_HIT_R` is half of `CELL_PITCH`
(`72`), so at most one cell center lies strictly within that radius, and a
position lying exactly `GEM_HIT_R` from two centers targets the cell at the
lower row, and within one row the cell at the lower column. A press farther than
`GEM_HIT_R` from every cell center targets no cell and changes nothing.

What a press does depends on the cell it targets and on whether a cell is
already selected. The rows below are evaluated in order, and the first row that
matches is the one that applies.

| Targeted cell | What the press does |
| --- | --- |
| Any cell, while nothing is selected | Selects that cell. |
| The selected cell | Clears the selection. |
| A cell orthogonally adjacent to the selected cell | Requests that swap and clears the selection. |
| Any other cell | Moves the selection to that cell. |

Dragging requests a swap without a second press. While the pointer is held down
from a press that targeted a cell, the pointer moving within `GEM_HIT_R` of the
center of a cell orthogonally adjacent to the pressed cell requests that swap
and clears the selection. A drag requests at most one swap, so the rest of that
hold changes nothing, and the release edge ends the drag.

Every requested swap goes through one acceptance path, whether a press, a drag,
or the keyboard asked for it, and the move rules in `specs/rules.md` decide it.

## The cursor

The keyboard drives a cursor over the board on the `playing` screen. The cursor
occupies one cell, and a round opens with it at `CURSOR_START_COL` (`0`),
`CURSOR_START_ROW` (`0`).

| Action | Where the cursor goes |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | One row toward row `0`. |
| `down` | One row toward row `GRID_ROWS - 1`. |
| `left` | One column toward column `0`. |
| `right` | One column toward column `GRID_COLS - 1`. |

The cursor stays on the board, so a movement that would carry it off an edge
leaves it where it is.

`confirm` acts on the cursor's cell exactly as a press on that cell does. The
four rows under Selecting and swapping decide it, read against the cursor's cell
in place of a targeted one.

## Actions and bindings

`ACTIONS` holds the actions the game registers with the runtime, and `BINDINGS`
maps each action to the keys that fire it. Each action is read once per frame as
a press edge.

| Action | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | Moves the menu highlight up, and the cursor up one row. |
| `down` | Moves the menu highlight down, and the cursor down one row. |
| `left` | Moves the cursor left one column. |
| `right` | Moves the cursor right one column. |
| `confirm` | Chooses the highlighted menu item; on the board, selects the cursor's cell, or swaps with it. |
| `pause` | Enters and leaves `paused` from `playing`. |
| `mute` | Toggles the runtime's mute bit. |
| `back` | Leaves `howto`, `paused`, and `gameover`. |

Every key is a `KeyboardEvent.code`, so a binding is a physical key rather than
a layout-dependent character.

| Action | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | `ArrowUp`, `KeyW` |
| `down` | `ArrowDown`, `KeyS` |
| `left` | `ArrowLeft`, `KeyA` |
| `right` | `ArrowRight`, `KeyD` |
| `confirm` | `Enter`, `Space` |
| `pause` | `KeyP` |
| `mute` | `KeyM` |
| `back` | `Escape` |

Each key listed for an action fires that action on its own, so either key of a
two-key action is enough. The screens these actions move between, and where each
one leads, are in `specs/ui.md`.
